[BlackTeam "Great Britain"]
[WhiteTeamCountry "CSR"]
[BlackTeamCountry "GBR"]

{My favourite game: RJ Broadbent, "Chess Treasury of The Air", Penguin 1966.
The game lingers in my memory for one reason in partcular. After a hard
struggle, the tension was eased by a series of exchanges, and there came about
the position [after White's 23rd]. Now although that position is on the face
of it quite sterile, offering little scope - to either player, it proved
possible to evolve an unusual kind of combination and thereby to open the door
to all kinds of tactical chances. That's one of the delights of chess: a
surprise may come at any moment. One is, of course, rather less than delighted
if the surprise proves to be an unpleasant one, but at least even that is
stimulating.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 d6 5. Nxd4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Be7
7. O-O O-O 8. b3 Re8 9. Bb2 Bf8 10. Re1 Bd7 11. Qd2 g6 12. Rad1 Bg7 13. Nxc6
Bxc6 14. f3 Qd7 15. a4 a6 16. a5 Re7 17. Nd5 Bxd5 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Qxd5 Qc6
20. Kf1 Qxd5 21. Rxd5 Bc3 22. Re3 Bb4 23. c3 {It is Black's turn to play his
23rd move, and the natural continuation would be Bc5, producing an evenly
balanced position in which it is hard to se how either side can make any
progress. But are there any other possibilities? Yes, perhaps the White rook
at d5 can be driven to a bad square. That is, in fact, the case, and it forms
the basis of Black's plan. Instead of Bc5 he plays} c6 (23... c6 {Now the
first point White has to note is that his rook cannot remain centrally
situated. For if} 24. Rd1 {there will follow} Bxa5 25. Rxd6 Bc7 {attacking the
White rook; and Black will pick up White's h-pawn. To be alert to that kind of
opportunity, it's helpful to remind oneself frequently of the pawns which are
unguarded; in this case, both of White's rook's pawns.}) 24. Rg5 Bc5 ({He
plays this quite happily, seeing that a further attack on his rook by} 24... h6
{could be refuted by} 25. Rxg6+) {to which White replies} 25. Rd3 {and here
comes the second point in Black's combination. He plays} Kg7 {; and now stands
ready to drive the White rook into obscurity. But there's a price to be paid
for this operation - one pawn - and White proceeds to exact it. He plays} 26.
b4 {and after Black's reply} Ba7 {he captures the Queen's pawn:} 27. Rxd6 {
The next few moves can, I think, be allowed to spek for themselves.} Be3 28.
Rg4 h5 29. Rh4 Bg5 30. Rh3 Bf4 31. Rd3 {Chess offers little scope for humour
but after all these years I still find this frog marching of the White rook
very amusing. A position is now reached which had to be foreseen by Black when
he embarked on his combinative plan eight moves earlier. It is seldom possible
to calculate accurately that many moves ahead; to do so there has to be a
pretty clear-cut continuation, with only a few alternatives to take into
account The normal limit, for me at any rate, is four or five moves ahead.
White has an extra pawn, but his king's rook is penned in and, for the time
being, more or less useless. It's going to be quite a job to bring it into
active play again. The question is whether in the meantime Black can use his
majority of effective pieces to win back a pawn and at the same time retain a
clear advantage in position. That question was beyond my power to settle
conclusively by analysis, even at this point; it was still more impossible
eight moves earlier. But it seemed pretty clear that Black, by advancing his
pawn to c5, could open a file for his rooks - either the 'c' or 'a' file. The
rooks could therefore find a way into the enemy position, and that seemed
highly likely, in my judgement, to yield a clear advantage. Therefore Black
now plays} Rc8 {and there follows} 32. g3 Bb8 33. Rd2 c5 34. Bd5 ({White's
best continuation here is probably} 34. b5 {with the idea of provoking a
series ofpawn exchanges. That would give him fewer weaklings to nurse.}) 34...
cxb4 35. cxb4 Rc1+ 36. Ke2 Rb1 {After this move White loses his b-pawn. And I
can sum up the rest of the gameby saying that after White's b-pawn had fallen,
he found himself unable, with a rook still out of play, to avoid further loss
of material. In fact, he resigned nine moves later. In conclusion I would just
say this. We all like to back a certain winner, and no one would hesitate to
offer, say, a pawn, if he knew with mathematical certainty that it would play
dividends. But I suggest that it is much more interesting and satisfying to
undertake such an operation when it has an element of uncertainty. One then
has to rely ultimately on a general judgement of the possibilities, and it is
just that which raises chess abve the level of a mere exercise in calculation.}
37. g4 hxg4 38. fxg4 Rxb4 39. Rc2 Bc7 40. Rc5 Rb2+ 41. Kd3 Rd7 42. Kc4 Be5 43.
Rb3 Rc2+ 44. Kb4 Bd6 45. Rbc3 Bxc5+ 0-1

